EXT. SCHOOL ROOFTOP - PIGEON COOP - DAWN

Tar paper and chimney pots. A lean-to coop of chicken wire and scrap plywood, thick with the soft burble of birds. Behind it, the city blushes pink under a rising sun.

MARISOL QUINONES, 16, in a school sweater with the sleeves pushed up, kneels beside the coop and shakes seed into a tin tray. ELI ABRAMOWITZ, 16, thin, round glasses, tries to climb through the roof hatch with a cardboard box held above his head. A single pigeon sits in the box, unimpressed.

ELI
Marisol. I brought the injured one.

MARISOL
You brought Gerald?

ELI
His name is Clementine.

The hatch slams on his foot. He hops. The box tips and the pigeon bursts out in a flurry of feathers. Marisol lunges and catches it against her chest while Eli topples back down the hatch, and a distant thump rises from the stairwell.

MARISOL
Eli?

ELI (O.S.)
I'm fine.

She cradles the bird. It coos. She smooths its wing with a thumb, and a tiny smile creeps over her face.
